"MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.05475)\nciteturn54academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate strong capabilities in general coding tasks but encounter two key challenges when optimizing code: (i) the complexity of writing optimized code (such as performant CUDA kernels and competition-level CPU code) requires expertise in systems, algorithms and specific languages and (ii) requires interpretation of performance metrics like timing and device utilization beyond binary correctness. ... Testing on the KernelBench (CUDA) and PIE (C++) optimization benchmarks shows that MaxCode improves optimized code performance compared to baselines, achieving 20.3% and 10.1% relative improvements in absolute speedup value and relative speedup ranking, respectively.\nTitle: MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization\nAuthors: Jiefu Ou, Sapana Chaudhary, Kaj Bostrom, Nathaniel Weir, Shuai Zhang, Huzefa Rangwala, George Karypis\nDate: Fri Jan  9 02:21:28 2026\n\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate strong capabilities in general coding tasks but encounter two key challenges when optimizing code: (i) the complexity of writing optimized code (such as performant CUDA kernels and competition-level CPU code) requires expertise in systems, algorithms and specific languages and (ii) requires interpretation of performance metrics like timing and device utilization beyond binary correctness. In this work, we explore inference-time search algorithms that guide the LLM to discover better solutions through iterative refinement based on execution feedback. Our approach, called MaxCode unifies existing search methods under a max-reward reinforcement learning framework, making the observation and action-value functions modular for modification. To enhance the observation space, we integrate a natural language critique model that converts raw execution feedback into diagnostic insights about errors and performance bottlenecks, and the best-discounted reward seen so far. Together, these provide richer input to the code proposal function. To improve exploration during search, we train a generative reward-to-go model using action values from rollouts to rerank potential solutions. Testing on the KernelBench (CUDA) and PIE (C++) optimization benchmarks shows that MaxCode improves optimized code performance compared to baselines, achieving 20.3% and 10.1% relative improvements in absolute speedup value and relative speedup ranking, respectively.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQing Wang | Nanjing University | Systems Research (https://wangqing.io/)\nciteturn54search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;   * [ASE'26]AlphaCu: A Transformation-Driven Synthesis Framework for LLM-based GPU Kernel Generation Yangjie Zhou, Xing Ma, Zihan Liu, Qing Wang, Weihao Cui, Wu Sun, Hanjing Wang, Binhang Qi, Jingwen Leng, Yun Lin, Jin Song Dong, Minyi Guo ...   * [SIGCOMM'26]Pegasus: A Data Center Network for Bare-Metal AI Cloud Xianneng Zou, Yadong Liu, Yiran Zhang, Jian Zhao, Zhaoxun Zhou, Qing Wang, Xingyi Li, Liang Wang, Xingda Wei, Xiaojie Huang, Zhaohe Chen, Yinben Xia, Yuxi Wang, Kai Ren, Lizhou Gao, Jiajun Liang, Jian Wang, Chunxu Zhao, Weifeng Zhang, Lu Shi, Xin Li, Jiewei Yang, Hao Lu, Yunpeng Guan, Dongbo Gu, Chao Pei, Xiang Li, Zekun He, Yong Jiang, Yachen Wang\n### 2026\n\n  * [FAST'26]Cost-efficient Archive Cloud Storage with Tape: Design and DeploymentQing Wang*, Fan Yang*, Qiang Liu, Geng Xiao, Yongpeng Chen, Hao Lan, Leiming Chen, Bangzhu Chen, Chenrui Liu, Pingchang Bai, Bin Huang, Zigan Luo, Mingyu Xie, Yu Wang, Youyou Lu, Huatao Wu, Jiwu Shu\nThe 24th USENIX Conference on File and Storage Technologies, 2026 (Best Paper Award Candidate)\n  * [ISCA'26]Breaking Barriers in Atomic Scaling: A Hardware-Software-Collaborated Framework to Deconstruct RDMA Atomic Guangyang Deng, Qiangsheng Su, Zhirong Shen, Qing Wang, Yina Lv, Ronglong Wu, Jiwu Shu\nProceedings of International Symposium on Computer Architecture, 2026\n  * [MICRO'26]Hera: Eliminating the I/O Tax of RAID on High-Capacity SSDs Jian Chen, Congming Gao, Fan Yang, Qing Wang, Zhengzhi Xin, Yuhao Zhang, Youyou Lu, Jiwu Shu\nThe 59th IEEE/ACM International Symposium on Microarchitecture, 2026\n  * [ASE'26]AlphaCu: A Transformation-Driven Synthesis Framework for LLM-based GPU Kernel Generation Yangjie Zhou, Xing Ma, Zihan Liu, Qing Wang, Weihao Cui, Wu Sun, Hanjing Wang, Binhang Qi, Jingwen Leng, Yun Lin, Jin Song Dong, Minyi Guo\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\npaper-notes/docs/automated-kernel-generation/maxcode.md at main · zksainx/paper-notes · GitHub (https://github.com/zksainx/paper-notes/blob/main/docs/automated-kernel-generation/maxcode.md)\nciteturn54search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization ... Reward model is inconsistent  | It underperforms plain MaxCode on KernelBench level 1 and lowers median speedup on PIE, so learned guidance is not yet reliable\n\n# MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization\n\nAuthors: Huzefa Rangwala, George Karypis\nInstitution: Johns Hopkins University, Amazon Web Services\nConference: arXiv\nYear: 2026\nPaper Link: arXiv:2601.05475\n\nCode Optimization Max-Reward RL Inference-Time Search\n\n## Background\n\nMaxCode studies optimization tasks where a language model starts from correct but slow code and must iteratively produce faster variants. This is harder than ordinary code generation because success depends on both functional correctness and hardware-sensitive performance. Raw execution feedback such as compile errors, test failures, or a single speedup number is often too weak to explain why a candidate is slow or what transformation should come next.\n\nThe key reframing is to treat code optimization as a max-reward reinforcement learning problem. The objective is not cumulative reward across all intermediate steps, but the best performance found anywhere along the search trajectory. That viewpoint matches kernel and systems optimization more naturally: many intermediate edits are only useful if they eventually lead to one high-quality candidate.\n\nKey Takeaways\n\n  * MaxCode extends iterative code optimization with a state that includes execution feedback, natural-language critique, and the best reward seen so far.\n  * The framework is modular: existing search methods such as Effi-Learner and CUDA-LLM can be rewritten under the same max-reward inference operator.\n  * A learned reward-to-go model is explored as an additional search guide, but its gains are mixed and sensitive to distribution shift.\n\n## Methodology\n\nMaxCode defines an extended MDP where each state is `s_t = (x_0, x_t, e_t, c_t)`: the original problem and seed implementation, the current candidate code, executor feedback, and a critique generated by a separate LLM. The system also maintains an auxiliary variable `u_t` for the best discounted reward found so far. This makes the search policy condition on both the current candidate and historical best performance, which standard iterative prompting does not encode explicitly.\n\nThe framework then approximates a max-reward inference operator over a fixed code-generation policy. In practice, this means sampling candidate rewrites, executing them, attaching critique, updating `u_t`, and selecting which branch to continue. MaxCode instantiates this idea by reformulating Effi-Learner and CUDA-LLM. It additionally trains a categorical reward-to-go model that predicts the best future reward achievable from a partial search trajectory and uses that prediction to rerank candidates before expensive execution.\n\n### System Overview\n\nComponent  | Input  | Output  | Role\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nExecutor `EX`  | Candidate code and test cases  | Correctness signal, performance signal, diagnostic feedback  | Provides the grounded reward used for search\nCritique Model `pi_c`  | Candidate code plus execution feedback  | Natural-language diagnosis and refinement hints  | Converts low-level feedback into actionable search context\nGenerator Policy `pi_theta`  | State `(x_0, x_t, e_t, c_t)` and best reward `u_t`  | New code revision  | Proposes the next optimization candidate\n  * Continuous speedups are bucketed into 5 reward categories, with dataset-specific thresholds for KernelBench level 1, KernelBench level 2, and PIE.\n  * The reward model is trained from rollout prefixes collected by MaxCode search, using Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct with LoRA as a lightweight generative value estimator.\n\n## Experiment\n\n### Experimental Setup\n\nItem  | Value\n--- | ---\nBenchmarks  | KernelBench (CUDA) and PIE (C++)\nKernelBench Scope  | Level 1 and Level 2, 100 problems each\nPIE Scope  | 68 sampled test problems\nGenerator / Critic LLM  | Claude-3.7-Sonnet\nTemperature  | 0.6 for code and critique generation\nEffi-Learner Budget  | Single-path refinement, depth = 2\nCUDA-LLM Budget  | Depth = 8, `K = 8` candidates\nReward Model  | Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct + LoRA rank 8\nMetrics  | Correctness, Fast1, median max speedup, average rank of max speedup\n\n### Headline Results\n\nMethod  | KernelBench L1  | KernelBench L2  | PIE\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nBest@64  | Rank 2.85, Median 1.00x  | Rank 2.15, Median 1.02x  | Rank 2.29, Median 1.32x\nEffi-Learner  | Rank 3.02, Median 1.00x  | Rank 2.98, Median 1.00x  | Rank 3.72, Median 1.00x\nEffi-Learner + MaxCode  | Rank 2.98, Median 1.00x  | Rank 2.95, Median 1.00x  | Rank 3.55, Median 1.03x\nCUDA-LLM  | Rank 1.54, Median 2.49x  | Rank 1.64, Median 1.45x  | Rank 2.05, Median 1.42x\nCUDA-LLM + MaxCode  | Rank 1.43, Median 3.17x  | Rank 1.51, Median 1.61x  | Rank 1.74, Median 1.74x\n\nThe strongest result comes from combining MaxCode with CUDA-LLM. Relative to vanilla CUDA-LLM, the paper reports median speedup gains of 27.3% on KernelBench level 1, 11.0% on level 2, and 22.5% on PIE. These gains align with the overall claim that critique plus best-so-far reward makes the search more sample-efficient under the same execution budget.\n\n### Reward-Guided Search\n\nSetting  | KernelBench L1  | KernelBench L2  | PIE\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nMaxCode  | Rank 1.40, Median 3.24x  | Rank 1.57, Median 1.22x  | Rank 1.55, Median 1.72x\nMaxCode + Reward  | Rank 1.53, Median 2.44x  | Rank 1.33, Median 1.24x  | Rank 1.43, Median 1.62x\n\nThe learned reward model helps some settings but does not produce a clean win. It improves ranking on KernelBench level 2 and PIE, slightly improves median speedup on level 2, but degrades median speedup on KernelBench level 1 and PIE. This makes the reward model more of an exploratory direction than a solved component.\n\n### Ablation Findings\n\n  * Trajectory history plus critique is the most consistently useful prompt augmentation.\n  * Adding only one auxiliary signal often yields limited gains and can slightly hurt performance.\n  * Under the same search depth, MaxCode reaches stronger median speedup earlier than vanilla CUDA-LLM, indicating better test-time scaling.\n\n## Limitation\n\nThe paper explicitly identifies reward-model accuracy and trajectory distribution shift as the main reasons reward-guided search remains unstable. The training rollouts use a different search dynamic from the guided inference procedure, so the learned value estimator is asked to generalize out of distribution.\n\nLimitation  | Why It Matters\n--- | ---\nReward model is inconsistent  | It underperforms plain MaxCode on KernelBench level 1 and lowers median speedup on PIE, so learned guidance is not yet reliable\nEvaluation scope is partial  | KernelBench experiments only cover levels 1 and 2, and PIE uses a 68-problem subset rather than the full benchmark\nSearch remains execution-bound  | Every branch still requires compilation and runtime evaluation, which constrains deeper exploration\nCritique quality is not isolated directly  | The paper shows that critique helps in combination with trajectory information, but it does not separately benchmark critique fidelity\n\n* * *\n\nReading date: 2026-04 Note status: Completed--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStatistical Characterization for Per-Hop QoS (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=31e14816b02c09f0c2ffd3b2dd443fe01959d514&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn54search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; the DiffAgent issues a Netlink socket call to the kernel with the appropriate instructions for setting up ... erating systems other than Linux, such as Cisco IOS, in which case, the DiffAgent maps the incoming\n1\r\n2\r\n3\r\n4\r\n5\r\n6\r\nTraffic\r\nAgent\r\nTraffic\r\nAgent\r\nTraffic\r\nAgent\r\nTraffic\r\nAgent\r\nTraffic\r\nAgent\r\nTraffic\r\nAgent\r\nM\r\nNetwork Configuration\r\nAgent\r\nH\r\nNI\r\nTraffic\r\nAgent\r\nController\r\nAgent\r\nRouter Configuration\r\nAgent\r\nswitch\r\nAnalysis\r\nModule\r\nS\r\nTraffic\r\nAgent\r\nRemote\r\nAgent\r\nFigure 4: Framework network\nthe Linux kernel [12]. Upon receipt of a conﬁguration message from its Network Conﬁguration agent,\nthe DiffAgent issues a Netlink socket call to the kernel with the appropriate instructions for setting up\nqueuing disciplines, classiﬁers and trafﬁc classes. This design allows inclusion of routers based on op-\nerating systems other than Linux, such as Cisco IOS, in which case, the DiffAgent maps the incoming\nmessages to OS-speciﬁc calls before transmitting them to the router.\nAnalysis Module performs ANOVA, model validation tests and polynomial regression on the experimental\noutput data. Once a set of experiments have been completed, the Controller agent invokes this module.\nThe outcome of the module is the set of functional relationships between factors and response variables.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - AI-Hypercomputer/accelerator-agents · GitHub (https://github.com/AI-Hypercomputer/accelerator-agents)\nciteturn54search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; This repository hosts agents that assist with code migration, kernel optimization, and performance tuning, enabling developers to leverage the full power of TPUs with greater velocity. ...   * CUDA to Pallas Conversion: Assists in porting custom CUDA/GPU kernels to run optimally on TPUs. ... A CPU-only VM is sufficient for MaxCode, while a TPU VM is recommended for MaxKernel.\n\nAI-Hypercomputer / accelerator-agents Public\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 9\n  * Star\n\n  * Code\n  * Issues 1\n  * Pull requests 18\n  * Actions\n  * Projects\n  * Security and quality 0\n  * Insights\n\n\n## Folders and files\n\nName  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n\n## Latest commit\n\n \n\n## History\n\n254 Commits\nJAXBench  | JAXBench  |    |  \nMaxCode  | MaxCode  |    |  \nMaxKernel  | MaxKernel  |    |  \ndocs  | docs  |    |  \nLICENSE  | LICENSE  |    |  \nREADME.md  | README.md  |    |  \n\n\n# Accelerator Agents\n\nAccelerator Agents is a collection of AI-powered tools designed to accelerate machine learning development on Google Cloud TPUs. This repository hosts agents that assist with code migration, kernel optimization, and performance tuning, enabling developers to leverage the full power of TPUs with greater velocity.\n  * MaxText Integration: Generates JAX code compatible with the MaxText framework for immediate training and inference on TPUs.\n  * Human-in-the-Loop: Designed to draft initial implementations that developers can review and refine.\n\n### 2. MaxKernel\n\nThe MaxKernel agent is a specialized tool for high-performance kernel development on TPUs. It assists engineers in writing, optimizing, and debugging custom kernels, specifically focusing on Pallas (JAX's kernel language).\n\nFeatures:\n\n  * Kernel Writing: Drafts Pallas kernels from scratch or based on JAX reference implementations.\n  * CUDA to Pallas Conversion: Assists in porting custom CUDA/GPU kernels to run optimally on TPUs.\n  * Optimization & Profiling: Provides profiling insights and optimization suggestions to improve kernel performance (MFU).\n  * Test Harness Generation: Automatically generates boilerplate code for correctness testing and compilation checks.\n\n## Getting Started\n\n### Prerequisites\n\n  * A Google Cloud VM. A CPU-only VM is sufficient for MaxCode, while a TPU VM is recommended for MaxKernel.\n  * Python 3.11+\n  * Access to Gemini API (for agent reasoning capabilities).\n\n### Installation\n\nClone the repository: `bash git clone https://github.com/AI-Hypercomputer/accelerator-agents.git cd accelerator-agents`\n\n(Note: Specific installation instructions for each agent can be found in their respective subdirectories.)\n\n### Contributing\n\nWe welcome contributions! Please see CONTRIBUTING.md for details on how to submit pull requests, report issues, and contribute to the project.\n\n### License\n\nThis project is licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. See LICENSE for the full license text.\n\n### Stars\n\n50 stars\n\n### Watchers\n\n0 watching\n\n### Forks\n\n9 forks--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - initiatesofzeus-design/maxcode: MaxCode — Self-evolving coding agent with multi-brain architecture, 3-tier memory, and autoresearch ratchet. Built on pi.dev. · GitHub (https://github.com/initiatesofzeus-design/maxcode)\nciteturn54search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; MaxCode combines multi-model orchestration, MemGPT-style 3-tier memory, hyperbolic embeddings, and a Karpathy-style autoresearch ratchet into a single system that gets better the more you use it. ...   * Node.js ≥ 20 ...   * GPU — ≥12GB VRAM recommended (RTX 3060+ or equivalent) ...     git clone https://github.com/initiatesofzeus-design/maxcode.git\n## Requirements\n\n  * Node.js ≥ 20\n  * pi.dev — coding agent harness (provides the architect LLM)\n  * Ollama — local LLM server for sentinel + embeddings\n  * GPU — ≥12GB VRAM recommended (RTX 3060+ or equivalent)\n\n### Ollama Models Needed\n    \n    ollama pull qwen2.5-coder:14b    # Sentinel (code review, verification)\n    ollama pull nomic-embed-text      # Embeddings (vector search)\n\n### Optional\n\n  * MiniMax API key — for the builder model (or swap for any OpenAI-compatible API)\n\n## Setup\n    \n    # 1. Clone this repo\n    git clone https://github.com/initiatesofzeus-design/maxcode.git\n    cd maxcode\n    \n    # 2. Install dependencies\n    npm install\n    \n    # 3. Configure environment\n    cp .env.example .env\n    # Edit .env with your API keys\n    \n    # 4. Start Ollama (if not running)\n    ollama serve\n    \n    # 5. Verify the build\n    node verify-build.mjs\n    \n    # 6. Run tests\n    npm test\n    \n    # 7. Run with pi.dev\n    pi   # From the maxcode directory\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - OpenGVLab/DiffAgent: [CVPR 2024] DiffAgent: Fast and Accurate Text-to-Image API Selection with Large Language Model · GitHub (https://github.com/opengvlab/diffagent)\nciteturn54search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago;     git clone https://github.com/OpenGVLab/DiffAgent.git\n\n# DiffAgent: Fast and Accurate Text-to-Image API Selection with Large Language Model\n\n## Abstract\n\nCLICK for the full abstract\n\n> Text-to-image (T2I) generative models have attracted significant attention and found extensive applications within and beyond academic research. For example, the Civitai community, a platform for T2I innovation, currently hosts an impressive array of 74,492 distinct models. However, this diversity presents a formidable challenge in selecting the most appropriate model and parameters, a process that typically requires numerous trials. Drawing inspiration from the tool usage research of large language models (LLMs), we introduce DiffAgent, an LLM agent designed to screen the accurate selection in seconds via API calls. DiffAgent leverages a novel two-stage training framework, SFTA, enabling it to accurately align T2I API responses with user input in accordance with human preferences. To train and evaluate DiffAgent's capabilities, we present DABench, a comprehensive dataset encompassing an extensive range of T2I APIs from the community. Our evaluations reveal that DiffAgent not only excels in identifying the appropriate T2I API but also underscores the effectiveness of the SFTA training framework.\n\nWe are open to any suggestions and discussions and feel free to contact us through liruizhao@stu.xmu.edu.cn.\n\n## TODO\n\n  * [Input] dataset\n  * [Input] data collection script\n  * [Input] pretrain model\n  * [Input] training code\n\n## News\n\n  * 2024/04/15 - Our dataset DABench is now publicly accessible and can be retrieved from Google Drive!\n\n## Contents\n\n  * Install\n  * Dataset\n  * Usage\n  * Citation\n\n## Install\n    \n    `conda create -n diffagent python=3.9.17\n    conda activate diffagent\n    git clone https://github.com/OpenGVLab/DiffAgent.git\n    cd diffagent\n    pip install -r requirements.txt\n    `\n\n## Dataset\n\nOur research introduces a high-quality dataset, DABench, accessible via Google Drive, encompassing Instruction-API pairs from SD 1.5 and SD XL (a total of 50,482). Additionally, we furnish the corresponding mapping dictionaries to facilitate subsequent model downloads or API information reconstruction.\n\nThe dataset DABench proposed in our work is collected from Civitai (license). The stipulations of the license highlight potential legal implications if this dataset is employed for commercial objectives. Therefore, it is strongly recommended that any entity intending to utilize this data for commercial ends should seek explicit authorization from either the relevant website or author.\n\n## Usage\n\n## Citation\n\nIf you use our work or our dataset in this repo, or find them helpful, please consider giving a citation.\n    \n    `@article{zhao2024diffagent,\n      title={DiffAgent: Fast and Accurate Text-to-Image API Selection with Large Language Model},\n      author={Zhao, Lirui and Yang, Yue and Zhang, Kaipeng and Shao, Wenqi and Zhang, Yuxin and Qiao, Yu and Luo, Ping and Ji, Rongrong},\n      journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2404.01342},\n      year={2024}\n    }\n    `\n\n## About\n\n[CVPR 2024] DiffAgent: Fast and Accurate Text-to-Image API Selection with Large Language Model\n\n### Resources\n\nReadme\n\n\n### Stars\n\n19 stars\n\n### Watchers\n\n0 watching\n\n### Forks\n\n0 forks--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - flagos-ai/awesome-LLM-driven-kernel-generation: Review automated kernel generation in the era of LLMs · GitHub (https://github.com/flagos-ai/awesome-LLM-driven-kernel-generation)\nciteturn54search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; [12/2025] PEAK: A Performance Engineering AI-Assistant for GPU Kernels Powered by Natural Language Transformations [paper] ... [01/2026] MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization [paper] ... [06/2025] IntelliPerf: Profiling-guided LLM framework for iterative GPU kernel optimization on AMD GPUs [blog] | [code] ... [09/2018] Nsight Compute — GPU Kernel Profiling Guide [docs]\n\n[01/2026] MaxCode: A Max-Reward Reinforcement Learning Framework for Automated Code Optimization [paper]\n\n[01/2026] AscendCraft: Automatic Ascend NPU Kernel Generation via DSL-Guided Transcompilation [paper]\n\n[02/2026] K-Search: LLM Kernel Generation via Co-Evolving Intrinsic World Model [paper] | [code]\n\n[03/2026] AutoKernel: Autonomous GPU Kernel Optimization via Iterative Agent-Driven Search [paper] | [code]\n\n[03/2026] KERNELFOUNDRY: HARDWARE-AWARE EVOLUTIONARY GPU KERNEL OPTIMIZATION [paper]\n\n[03/2026] AVO: Agentic Variation Operators for Autonomous Evolutionary Search [paper]\n\n[03/2026] AKO: Agentic Kernel Optimization (a harness for existing coding agents) [project] | [code]\n\n[04/2026] CuTeGen: An LLM-Based Agentic Framework for Generation and Optimization of High-Performance GPU Kernels using CuTe [paper]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiffAgent: Fast and Accurate Text-to-Image API Selection with Large Language Model (https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.01342)\nciteturn54academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; DiffAgent leverages a novel two-stage training framework, SFTA, enabling it to accurately align T2I API responses with user input in accordance with human preferences.\nTitle: DiffAgent: Fast and Accurate Text-to-Image API Selection with Large Language Model\nAuthors: Lirui Zhao, Yue Yang, Kaipeng Zhang, Wenqi Shao, Yuxin Zhang, Yu Qiao, Ping Luo, Rongrong Ji\nDate: Sun Mar 31 06:28:15 2024\n\nText-to-image (T2I) generative models have attracted significant attention and found extensive applications within and beyond academic research. For example, the Civitai community, a platform for T2I innovation, currently hosts an impressive array of 74,492 distinct models. However, this diversity presents a formidable challenge in selecting the most appropriate model and parameters, a process that typically requires numerous trials. Drawing inspiration from the tool usage research of large language models (LLMs), we introduce DiffAgent, an LLM agent designed to screen the accurate selection in seconds via API calls. DiffAgent leverages a novel two-stage training framework, SFTA, enabling it to accurately align T2I API responses with user input in accordance with human preferences. To train and evaluate DiffAgent's capabilities, we p